{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":5,"timestamp":1786894776,"nonce":"W9JXD7mMop12diZ+njBwTklk0Ng=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABM9vGx88AAAAG394ghw"},"added":["bug"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1786908385,"nonce":"OoauEhVKEzkFD4qVHYLPK9GftdQ=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABPHTCAw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/109#issuecomment-5309252099"},"message":"## Root cause: the objective was replaced, not broken\n\n#97 did not break the descent — it **changed what the descent minimises**, and the old capability was removed rather than renamed.\n\n- **Before (#94)**, `run_adaptive_relocate` moved the tagged vertices *down the FE Dirichlet-energy gradient*. Its own docstring: \"Relocate the mesh vertices tagged with `Variable.trainable` down the FE Dirichlet-energy gradient … `fem.adapt_history` traces the per-step energy.\"\n- **After (#97)**, descent walks down the **equidistribution defect of an arclength monitor** (`_equidistribution_jax`), with `\"huang\"` and `\"monge_ampere\"` as alternatives. There is no energy option.\n\nThat explains the sign. The tutorial scores r-adaptivity by Dirichlet energy, which is not an arbitrary choice: for a Ritz method `E_h - E_exact = ½‖u - u_h‖²_E`, so minimising the energy at fixed DOFs *is* minimising the energy-norm error. The old objective optimised exactly that. The monitor functionals are proxies, and on this problem all three move the mesh in a direction that increases the energy.\n\nMeasured on the tutorial's L-shape (coarse error 4.459e-03; the assertion wants \u003c 0.75×):\n\n| objective | energy error | vs coarse |\n|---|---|---|\n| `equidistribution` (the default) | 4.982e-03 | **−12%** |\n| `huang` | 4.525e-03 | −1% |\n| `monge_ampere` | 4.645e-03 | −4% |\n| energy-gradient descent (as of `9ecdfb6`) | 1.991e-03 | **+55%** |\n\n## A second, smaller finding\n\n`AdaptSpec.objective` accepts `\"equidistribution\"` / `\"huang\"`, but **`jno.solve.relocate()` exposes no `objective=` parameter** — so the knob is unreachable from the public front door. The table above needed `dataclasses.replace` on the spec to test it. Either surface it or drop it.\n\n## What the fix probably is\n\nNot \"revert #97\". The monitor approach is standard in the moving-mesh literature and the docstring records it winning on an Allen–Cahn front (`descent` at 0.361× uniform), which is a *feature-tracking* problem rather than a fixed-singularity one. So the two objectives suit different problems and the honest resolution is a choice:\n\n1. **Restore energy descent as an objective** (`objective=\"energy\"`), and make it the default where it belongs — it is the right thing to minimise for a steady Ritz problem, and it is what the tutorial documents.\n2. **Or** keep the monitor default, and change what r-adaptivity *claims*: the tutorial's assertion, and the docs, currently promise an energy reduction the default no longer delivers.\n\nEither way the missing guard is the same: the \"does relocation cut the error at fixed DOFs\" measurement lives only in a tutorial, which is why a change of objective could land without anything going red.","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1791535243,"nonce":"oPCoQIJDU033XbMJWam0FG3e4e8=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABaj9PLw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/109#issuecomment-6077501231"},"message":"Fixed on main by #111 (271363d3). The energy objective is back as `jno.solve.relocate(objective=\"energy\")` (the knob is now reachable from the public slot), and the property this tutorial used to be the only guard for is pinned by tests:\n\n- `tests/test_fem_relocate.py::test_energy_descent_cuts_the_error_at_fixed_dofs_on_a_fixed_singularity`\n- `tests/test_fem_relocate.py::test_the_two_objectives_each_win_on_their_own_problem`\n- `tests/test_fem_relocate.py::test_the_objective_is_reachable_from_the_public_slot_and_validated`\n\nRe-run on main (1e914634): all pass. The default stays the equidistribution monitor (option 2 in the root-cause comment); the tutorial now scores the default by a mesh-quality assert rather than an energy-cut claim. The remaining source-term caveat of `objective=\"energy\"` is tracked in #114.","files":null},{"type":4,"timestamp":1791535244,"nonce":"SXtcn8YutFg4I1iqR6F+CvP8Z3U=","metadata":{"github-id":"CE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABM9vGx88AAAAHppiWUw"},"status":2},{"type":3,"timestamp":1791537344,"nonce":"Aq03Aq8yw5FqvdBnUpIy8ISGOR4=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABakdCwQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/109#issuecomment-6078022337"},"message":"Correction to my closing comment: reopening. The tests I cited do not pin what they claim.\n\n- `test_energy_descent_cuts_the_error_at_fixed_dofs_on_a_fixed_singularity` and `test_the_two_objectives_each_win_on_their_own_problem` score the error as `E_h - E_ref`. With a source and a homogeneous wall, E_h approaches E from **below**, so that number is negative and grows more negative as the error grows. Measured on the test's own problem: `objective=\"energy\"` made the true energy error **6x worse** (6.21e-4 -\u003e 3.74e-3) and the test passed.\n- Their movable set (`0 \u003c x, y \u003c 1`) also frees both axes of the L-shape's two notch edges, so relocation reshaped the domain (E_h ended 36% above the converged L-shape's).\n\nThe underlying defect is #114's: `objective=\"energy\"` descended `a(u_h, u_h)` whatever the load, which with a source is `-2 J`. Fixed on branch `fix/open-issues` (94ac1bb3): the objective is now the form's Ritz functional, and on the fixed domain it cuts the error to 0.76x where the default raises it to 1.31x; both tests are corrected. This issue closes with that PR.","files":null},{"type":4,"timestamp":1791537345,"nonce":"IxLbIzmstATcVkC98ja0n0JE6YQ=","metadata":{"github-id":"REE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABM9vGx88AAAAHprqyjw"},"status":1},{"type":4,"timestamp":1791885631,"nonce":"DlzB8vURnnpHkUFYmFPVcmbEdzY=","metadata":{"github-id":"CE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABM9vGx88AAAAHtjSXBw"},"status":2}]}